== The one failure, PMPSm_cfg_A_tor_zero-00 (unchanged; the known PA0 platform waiver)
Same verdict and cycle count on both RTLs (FAIL, 18,024 cycles; the 31a15689 run and perf-56-gates also
recorded 18,024). Rerun alone on both core_tb builds:
  rdv: CORE TB FAIL act-elfs/elfs/priv/PMPSm/PMPSm_cfg_A_tor_zero-00.elf test 1 (htif, 18024 cycles)
  main: CORE TB FAIL act-elfs/elfs/priv/PMPSm/PMPSm_cfg_A_tor_zero-00.elf test 1 (htif, 18024 cycles)
  core_tb --trace (per-cycle fetch/bus/response/store log, 18025 lines): byte-identical between branch and main
Why the PMP fix cannot touch it: the test (tests/priv/PMPSm/PMPSm_cfg_A_tor_zero-00.S, cp_cfg_A_tor0) sets
entry 0 = L|TOR|XWR over [0, TEST_FOR_EXECUTION) and probes sw, lw and jalr at the top, top-4 and PA 0, all
in M-mode with satp bare: 4-byte data accesses and fetches. The fix only changes 8-byte data accesses
(mem_size_code == 3) and PTE accesses; fetches stay one word.
Why it fails (against Sail): the expected trap signature built into the ELF by the Sail run has three
records, final_trap_sig_offset 0x90 (objdump of this ELF):
   800130d0 0df0dec0 f50f117a 90000000 00000000  .......z........
   800130e0 5de2478b 6c1fa9d3 73060003 00000000  ].G.l...s.......
   800130f0 07000000 00000000 70010080 00000000  ........p.......
   80013100 00000000 00000000 00000000 00000000  ................
   80013110 00000000 00000000 73060003 00000000  ........s.......
   80013120 05000000 00000000 10020080 00000000  ................
   80013130 00000000 00000000 00000000 00000000  ................
   80013140 00000000 00000000 73060003 00000000  ........s.......
   80013150 01000000 00000000 00000000 00000000  ................
  i.e. cause 7 (store access fault) at 0x80000170 = PMPSm_cp_cfg_A_tor0_region0_3_sw_zero,
       cause 5 (load access fault)  at 0x80000210 = PMPSm_cp_cfg_A_tor0_region0_6_lw_zero,
       cause 1 (instruction access fault) at PA 0 (jalr_zero target).
Sail has no memory at PA 0, so all three PA-0 probes fault there although PMP entry 0 allows them. In
core_tb, PA 0 is RAM (cacheable [0, 0x1000_0000) for the directed tests), so the sw writes 0x8067 there,
the lw reads it back, and the jalr executes it (ret): no trap. Every per-probe register check passes
(x14 = 0x8067 on both: Sail's faulting lw leaves the earlier 0x8067, the core loads the 0x8067 its sw
wrote); only the trap-signature length check (check_trap_sig_offset) fails, expected 0x90 vs 0. Commit trace
(+sigil_trace, branch core_tb) at the failing compare:
  I 4975 3 00000000800009d8 00000013
  I 4977 3 00000000800009dc 00000013
  I 4980 3 00000000800009e0 00043203 x4=0000000000000090
  I 4982 3 00000000800009e4 00620c63
  I 4983 3 00000000800009e8 2f8033ef x7=00000000800009ec
  I 4998 3 0000000080003ce0 0000d497 x9=0000000080010ce0
  (x4 = expected trap-signature bytes 0x90, loaded from 0x800130d8; x6 = trap-signature bytes the core wrote, 0
   (Mtrap_sig pointer minus trap_sigptr, sub at 0x800009a0); beq x4, x6 at 0x800009e4 not taken, so jal x7 to failedtest_trap_x7_x9)
The core_tb bus trace shows the jalr_zero fetch at PA 0 (cycle 3068) returning 0x00008067 (ret) and no trap.
This is the PA0 platform waiver recorded since act-core-impriv-2026-10-13 (docs/evidence/README.md).

